hat he made on the  District of Columbia Court of Appeals  we have every confidence that as members  of the Senate come to know and  appreciate judge Cavanaugh as the  American people come to know and  appreciate this principle jurist that  we'll see him confirm you know there's a  huge pushback from the left  Gillibrand Freedom this just must not  become the next justice on the Supreme  Court  what President Trump was looking for was  a judge with the background the  temperament and the experience who we  would be confident would faithfully  interpret the Constitution as written  the president wanted to put someone on  the bench who would not legislate from  the bench who would not as has been to  much of the case in the last generation  or more where we've had members of our  federal judiciary who literally have  been making policy and making law from  the bench president looked looked at  this judge and all of the candidates for  this opening through that prism and that  was the basis of this decision here's  the other argument sent a minority  leader today about this choice Senator  McConnell told the president that there  were other nominees who could have been  for affirmed more easily why did the  president stick with Kavanagh because  he's worried  [Applause]  that mr. Muller will go to the court and  ask that the president be subpoenaed and  asked to do other things necessary to  move the investigation forward and  president Trump knows that Cavanaugh  will be a barrier to preventing that  investigation from going there this  decision has anything to do with the  Muller investigation I I don't believe  it did in any way what what the  president focused on was a judicial  philosophy what he focused on was a body  of work by Judge Cavanaugh and we  examined other candidates who were  finalists for this position to see if we  could end that body of work determined  that this was a judge who would would  faithfully uphold and interpret the  Constitution as written it it really was  the lodestar in the course of all of  this now look make no mistake about it  the judge Cavanaugh is a is a he's a  he's a conservative jurist he's a  principled conservative in the tradition  of Justice Scalia when you look at cases  that he's ruled on on limited government  on federalism on the Second Amendment on  religious liberty in one case after  another you see a consistent judicial  philosophy come forward we're obviously  looking at all of the reaction and when  this vote will come together but there  are other things happening namely the  meeting in NATO the president heading  overseas and then the summit with  Vladimir Putin the present was asked  whether Vladimir's a foe or a friend  tipless I really can't say right now as  far as I'm concerned  competitor to competitor I think that  getting along with Russia getting along  with China getting along with others  it's a good thing not a bad thing I've  said that many times I have the UK which  is in somewhat turmoil and a half foot  thankfully Putin may be the easiest of  them all who would think what would  think so we're putting a friend or foe  the president made it clear that we have  competing interests with Russia but it  doesn't change the fact whether it be  Syria where Russia's involved militarily  on behalf of the Assad regime where our  military forces have bravely been in the  process of destroying Isis at its source  that we need to find a way to work in  common cause for a long-term solution in  Syria we have the we have the issues in  Ukraine other issues that are exist  between our countries and as president  Trump has said many times he always  believes in talking he believes in  sitting down with leaders in determining  whether there's a way that we can make  progress in the relationship and I think  he goes into this summit very much in  that spirit but in the wake of what  happened in North Korea and what we're  seeing so far is there a skepticism  about this you 